# Deepcore — The assets you produce

Deepcore ships no pre-made art, effects, or sound. Six asset-generation tools sit
on the `PATH` while this build is being made, and every sprite, animation,
particle system, and sound the game plays is produced with them, committed to this
repository, and loaded by the game at runtime. This file is the contract: what to
produce, which tool makes it, where the file lands, and how it is wired in.

The animated prospector is the headline. Its cycles carry as much of the finished
game as the simulation does.

## The six tools

| Tool | Produces | Used for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `draw` | One sprite, as a PNG | Tiles, ore, materials, hazards, buildings, the rocket, icons |
| `draw-sheet` | A sprite sheet, one PNG per frame | The miner's cycles, the lava shimmer, the crack overlay, the rocket assembly |
| `particle-2d` | A particle system, as a `system.json` | Every effect |
| `sfx-synth` | A synthesized sound, as a `.wav` | Cues built from synth voices |
| `sfx-sample` | A sampled sound over the sample pack, as a `.wav` | Richer impact, pickup, and confirm cues |
| `music` | Sequenced music over an instrument bank, as a `.wav` and a `.mid` | The music bed |

Each is a command-line tool. Run `<tool> --help` for its operations, and
`<tool> <operation> --help` for one operation's flags. In outline, each records
the operations run against it and then renders the finished file. `draw-sheet` is
`draw` with a `--frame <index>` on every operation, emitting one separate PNG per
frame; frames are separate files rather than regions of one image. `particle-2d`
authors a system of emitters, forces, and per-particle curves that is simulated
live, rather than baking frames. `sfx-sample` draws on the `combat-core` sample
pack and `music` on the `gm-lite` and `synthwave` instrument banks, all present in
the container and browsed with `list-samples` and `list-instruments`; `sfx-synth`
needs neither.

## The build is self-contained

Producing the assets is a one-time step of this build. `npm run build` bundles the
committed files and never invokes the tools, which are not present when the built
site is rebuilt from the committed source.

## Loading

The built site is served from an arbitrary base path, so no asset is referenced by
a root-absolute URL. Every produced file is referenced relative to the document or
the module that loads it, and the bundler's base is relative.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
Loading the produced files is part of the runtime layer you write.
{{else}}
The {{engine.name}} engine loads assets from a root you give it and reports
progress and failures as it goes; read its documentation in `engine/` for its
loader and use it rather than fetching the files yourself.
{{/if}}

## The layout

Every produced file lands under `assets/` at these exact paths.

| Path | Holds |
| --- | --- |
| `assets/miner/<state>/frameNN.png` | One frame of a miner cycle, numbered from `frame00.png`. |
| `assets/tiles/<band>-<n>.png` | A rock variant for `topsoil`, `rockbed`, `deepstone`, or `coreshell`. |
| `assets/tiles/bedrock.png` | The bedrock border. |
| `assets/tiles/stone-<n>.png` | An unbreakable boulder variant. |
| `assets/tiles/tunnel.png` | The carved-tunnel interior fill. |
| `assets/tiles/crack/frameNN.png` | One frame of the drill-damage overlay. |
| `assets/hazards/lava/frameNN.png` | One frame of the lava shimmer. |
| `assets/ore/<name>.png` | The overlay for one ore or gemstone, named in lower case. |
| `assets/materials/<name>.png` | `resonite`, `cryenite`, `core`, and `core-sample`. |
| `assets/surface/<id>.png` | One of the six buildings, by its id, plus `cave-mouth`, `ground`, and `sky`. |
| `assets/rocket/stageN.png` | The rocket with `N` components installed, `stage0` through `stage5`. |
| `assets/icons/<name>.png` | A status-bar icon. |
| `assets/fx/<name>.json` | One produced particle system. |
| `assets/audio/<name>.wav` | One produced sound or the music bed. |

Tiles, ore and gemstone overlays, material nodes, hazard cells, and the crack
overlay are authored at `TILE` (`80`) by `TILE` and drawn at that size. The miner
is authored to fit within `MINER_SPRITE` (`80`) by `MINER_SPRITE`. Icons are
`ICON_SIZE` (`24`) square. Overlays are authored on a transparent canvas with
straight alpha and sampled nearest-neighbor so they stay crisp.

## The miner

Produce one `draw-sheet` cycle per animation state, one PNG per frame, under
`assets/miner/<state>/`. The state names are the miner's
animation states.
The game plays the cycle matching the miner's current state, advancing frames at
`ANIM_FPS` (`12`) frames per second, looping every cycle but `hurt`, which plays
once. Every produced cycle carries at least `DRAWINGS_MIN` (`2`) distinct
drawings, so a cycle that repeats one picture is not a cycle.

| State | Minimum frames | The frame must carry |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `idle` | `2` | Standing at rest, with a breathing bob and a lamp flicker so the miner is alive. |
| `walk` | `4` | A readable walk on the ground, legs and drill swinging. |
| `drill-down` | `3` | Braced downward, the drill biting the floor, the body shaking with the cut. |
| `drill-side` | `3` | Braced sideways, the drill biting the wall ahead. |
| `jetpack` | `3` | Thrusting, the body lifted, the flame flickering at the nozzle. |
| `fall` | `2` | Dropping through open space, arms and legs trailing. |
| `hurt` | `2` | A sharp flinch and recoil at the instant of damage. |
| `fuel-out` | `2` | Slumped and powerless, the jetpack dead. |

Draw the miner facing one canonical direction and mirror it in the game to face
the other, so the silhouette, the suit, the drill, and the jetpack are identical
in both facings and across every cycle. The effort escalates across the set: the
idle is gentle, the drill shakes, the jetpack strains, the fall is limp, and the
hurt snaps.

## Environment sprites

Produce each of the following with `draw`.

- Band rock, at least `TILE_VARIANTS` (`3`) interchangeable variants per band,
  chosen per cell so a wall of one band never repeats a single stamp. Variants
  share the band's color and differ only in the grain. The grain is fine and even
  across the whole tile rather than a few large blotches.
- The bedrock border tile.
- Unbreakable stone, at least `STONE_VARIANTS` (`2`) variants, reading as a
  smooth, cold, harder material against the grainy band rock, with a distinct
  silhouette rather than a re-tinted dirt tile.
- The carved-tunnel interior fill, which the game paints inside the shaped tunnel.
- One overlay per ore, drawn as a smear of mineral run through the dirt: a streak
  spreading across much of the cell, feathering into the rock at the edges so the
  band rock shows through, and reaching the cell's edges so adjacent ore cells read
  as one continuous vein. An ore is never a discrete nugget sitting on the rock.
- One overlay per gemstone, drawn as a cut, faceted jewel with a flat table,
  angled facets, and a bright glint, unmistakably unlike the ore smear.
- Resonite and Cryenite nodes, as raw crystal embedded in rock, distinct from both
  ore and gemstones; the glowing Core in its chamber; and the Core Sample.
- The six surface buildings, each reading clearly as what it is, the cave mouth,
  the camp ground, and the sky backdrop above it.
- The rocket at each of its six assembly states, from the bare pad to the
  launch-ready rocket, so the player reads the win progress from the pad.
- The status-bar icons for fuel, hull, cargo, Credits, depth, and each material.

## Produced animations

- The lava shimmer: a looping cycle of at least `LAVA_FRAMES` (`2`) frames for a
  lava cell's molten interior, so lava glows and churns rather than sitting flat.
- The drill-damage crack overlay: at least `CRACK_FRAMES` (`4`) transparent frames
  running front to back from faint hairlines to a shattered, about-to-break face.
  The game picks the frame from a cell's damage fraction, `1 - health /
  BAND_HEALTH`, and draws it over every visible damaged cell, not only the one
  being cut.
- The rocket assembly, if the six states are produced as a sheet rather than as
  separate sprites.

## Particle systems

Author each effect with `particle-2d` and play it live, so it varies shot to shot.
A flat opacity flash or a hand-coded loop in place of a produced system does not
satisfy this contract.

| File | Fires when | Must carry |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `gas-seep.json` | Ambient, over each on-screen gas pocket in turn | A faint wisp of pale gas rising from the cell, from varied points across its face. Subtle enough that a hurried dig misses it, opaque enough to read against the rock when watched. |
| `drill-debris.json` | The miner is cutting a cell | A spray of chips and dust off the bit, tinted to the band. |
| `jetpack-exhaust.json` | The miner is thrusting | A downward plume of hot exhaust and sparks, pulsing with the hold. |
| `ore-sparkle.json` | An ore or gemstone is collected | A brief bright glint at the pickup, tinted to the ore. |
| `material-shimmer.json` | A material node is collected | A richer, distinct shimmer that reads as a prize rather than routine ore. |
| `gas-explosion.json` | A gas pocket detonates | A violent burst larger than the cell: a hard flash, a fast shockwave ring, an outward shell, and flying debris. |
| `lava-embers.json` | The miner touches lava | A sizzle of embers and smoke at the contact point. |
| `impact-dust.json` | The miner lands above the safe speed | A puff of dust at the touchdown. |
| `core-extract.json` | A Core Sample is taken | An ominous pulse of energy off the Core as the timer starts. |
| `core-detonation.json` | A Core Sample detonates | A huge explosion, much larger than a gas blast. |
| `launch-exhaust.json` | The rocket launches | A roaring column of exhaust and smoke under the rising rocket. |
| `death-burst.json` | The miner dies | A burst of venting suit and debris. |

Spawn an instance of the matching system at the event's position and let it play
out: the debris at the bit, the exhaust under the jetpack, the sparkle at the
pickup, the blast at the pocket, the launch exhaust under the rocket.

`@clockwyrks/particle-runtime` is already a dependency of this project, resolved
by a `file:` entry in `package.json`. Import it like any other dependency and do
not fetch or reimplement it. Its `/canvas` binding plays a parsed `system.json`
into a 2D drawing context, advanced by the frame's delta time; its own types are
the authoritative description of that API.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The context is the one your renderer draws into.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
The context is the one the engine hands the game to draw into.
{{else}}
The engine's declarative pipeline does not draw particles, so the systems are
played through a component that draws directly with the context the engine hands
it, at the layer the effects belong on.
{{/if}}

The explosives and a jettisoned Sample's ground detonation reuse
`gas-explosion.json` and `core-detonation.json`; the teleporters and the repair
supplies reuse the extraction, shimmer, and confirm cues. The field supplies need
no produced asset of their own.

## Audio

Produce every sound with `sfx-synth` or `sfx-sample`, and the music bed with
`music`. `music` emits a `.wav` alongside a `.mid`; the `.wav` is what plays.

| Cue | File | Plays |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `drill` | `assets/audio/drill.wav` | Looping while the miner is cutting. |
| `thrust` | `assets/audio/thrust.wav` | Looping while the jetpack fires. |
| `ore-pickup` | `assets/audio/ore-pickup.wav` | An ore or gemstone is banked. |
| `material-chime` | `assets/audio/material-chime.wav` | A material is banked. Richer than the ore pickup. |
| `gas-explosion` | `assets/audio/gas-explosion.wav` | A gas pocket detonates. |
| `lava-sizzle` | `assets/audio/lava-sizzle.wav` | The miner touches lava. |
| `impact` | `assets/audio/impact.wav` | The miner lands above the safe speed. |
| `fabricate` | `assets/audio/fabricate.wav` | A purchase or a fabrication succeeds. |
| `launch` | `assets/audio/launch.wav` | The rocket launches. |
| `death` | `assets/audio/death.wav` | The miner dies. |
| `alarm-fuel` | `assets/audio/alarm-fuel.wav` | Fuel is below `LOW_FUEL_FRACTION`. |
| `alarm-core` | `assets/audio/alarm-core.wav` | A Core Sample's timer runs, escalating as it nears zero. |
| `music` | `assets/audio/music.wav` | Looping under the whole game. A lonely, industrial descent bed. |

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
Decode and play each file through the Web Audio API. Do not start audio before
the player has interacted with the page, since browsers block it, and provide the
mute toggle the status bar carries.
{{else}}
Define each cue by the name above with the engine's audio and play it by that
name; read its documentation in `engine/`. The engine holds the mute toggle and
the unlock on first interaction.
{{/if}}

## What the game draws in code

There is no tool for interface art, so all of the following are drawn in code:

- The whole status bar: the gauges, the readouts, the layout, and the text. Its
  icons are produced sprites; everything around them is code.
- Every menu, panel, and overlay screen.
- The scanner indicator and the Core Sample countdown.
- The field-supply icons.
- The faint tile grid over the produced rock.
- The carved-tunnel shaping and the lava fringe. A carved cell is drawn inset with
  a lip of the band's dirt and rounded corners, so a tunnel is narrower than a full
  cell; orthogonally adjacent open cells join into one passage and cells touching
  only at a corner stay separate. An exterior corner rounds convex; at an L-bend or
  a T-junction, where solid rock pokes diagonally into the bend, the dirt keeps a
  convex nub bulging into the tunnel rather than a scooped-out notch. A lava cell
  is fringed with the same dirt border, and adjacent lava cells flow together into
  one pool.
- The screen shake: a short render-space jitter of the world on a gas detonation,
  an explosives blast, a hard landing, or the Core Sample's detonation, scaled to
  the event and decaying out. The jitter displaces the drawn world by at least
  `SHAKE_MIN` (`2`) logical units at its peak, and the world is back where it
  started within `SHAKE_SETTLE` (`3`) seconds of the event. It is purely visual
  and never moves the simulation.
- The first-time hazard notice card.

## The bar

Every produced asset is genuinely produced with these tools. A build that ships
placeholder rectangles, code-drawn shapes in place of a produced sprite, a single
static frame in place of a miner cycle, a flat flash in place of a produced
particle system, downloaded art, or silence has not done this work, however good
the simulation is. Everything the game shows and plays traces either to a file
produced here or to the interface drawn in code above.
